WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:04.440
All right.

00:04.440 --> 00:05.960
So now what is a unique index.

00:06.120 --> 00:12.440
Unique index is a special type of indexes that can make sure no duplicates in your data.

00:12.440 --> 00:16.520
And there are a couple of reasons why is it important to have a unique index.

00:16.560 --> 00:20.800
The first one and the most obvious reason is to have data integrity.

00:20.800 --> 00:24.840
So the unique index can go and enforce uniqueness in your data.

00:24.840 --> 00:26.240
And that is very helpful.

00:26.240 --> 00:32.920
For example, if you have a column like an email address or a product ID, having duplicate in such

00:32.920 --> 00:35.880
columns can mess up your data very badly.

00:35.880 --> 00:42.200
So having a unique index on a column like an email can make sure there are no sneaky duplicates inside

00:42.200 --> 00:42.960
your data.

00:42.960 --> 00:48.560
And the second important reason why unique index is important is to improve the performance.

00:48.720 --> 00:53.880
So for example, if you are searching for a specific email, the SQL can start searching for the email

00:53.880 --> 00:54.520
value.

00:54.520 --> 01:00.800
And once the SQL find the value, the SQL will stop searching because we are sure that there is no duplicates

01:00.800 --> 01:01.640
in the data.

01:01.640 --> 01:05.120
So with that, you are improving the performance of your queries.

01:05.120 --> 01:08.560
So if you are creating an index and you know this column is unique.

01:08.600 --> 01:11.360
Then make sure to make the index as unique.

01:11.360 --> 01:11.880
Index.

01:11.920 --> 01:16.320
So now if you have a look again to our clustered index where we have the B structure.

01:16.320 --> 01:22.520
If you make this index as unique then you are giving an extra task for the SQL that's going to go.

01:22.520 --> 01:28.080
And make sure that all those IDs of the customer are going to be unique.

01:28.160 --> 01:34.640
So SQL has to guarantee that there are no duplicates at all inside your data in the database.

01:35.080 --> 01:41.920
So now since we are giving SQL an extra task to prove the uniqueness of the data, building the clustered

01:41.920 --> 01:44.040
index can be a little bit slower.

01:44.040 --> 01:46.120
So that means inserting new data.

01:46.120 --> 01:50.480
Writing data can be slower as the normal clustered index.

01:50.480 --> 01:55.040
But now if you are talking about the read performance, the performance of our query, it's going to

01:55.040 --> 01:59.840
be optimized a little bit faster than a normal clustered index.

01:59.840 --> 02:07.200
So again this trade off we are making writing data slower but we are gaining more speed on the query

02:07.200 --> 02:08.080
performance.

02:08.080 --> 02:10.600
So this is what we mean with unique index.

02:10.640 --> 02:10.880
Okay.

02:10.920 --> 02:13.600
So let's keep extending the syntax of the index.

02:13.600 --> 02:18.550
So now in order to tell whether it is unique or not, we can specify it exactly at the start.

02:18.790 --> 02:23.470
So we say create unique is just before the clustered or non-clustered.

02:23.470 --> 02:24.910
And then afterward the cluster.

02:24.910 --> 02:26.630
And nothing changed for the rest.

02:26.630 --> 02:30.190
So we can specify this keyword to tell SQL it should be unique.

02:30.390 --> 02:35.630
And if you don't write anything before the clustered index it's going to be not unique.

02:35.630 --> 02:39.470
So for example this one says create an index.

02:39.470 --> 02:41.550
So we didn't specify anything here.

02:41.590 --> 02:43.830
Duplicates are allowed in the index.

02:43.830 --> 02:48.990
But if you go and specify a unique index then the duplicates are not allowed.

02:48.990 --> 02:50.390
So it is very simple.

02:50.430 --> 02:50.710
Okay.

02:50.750 --> 02:53.190
So now let's go and create unique cluster.

02:53.430 --> 02:55.510
Now let's go and target the table products.

02:55.510 --> 02:55.910
Let's go.

02:55.910 --> 02:59.270
And first select the data from the table.

02:59.270 --> 03:03.070
So sales products and execute it.

03:03.110 --> 03:07.990
Now let's see that I'm going to go and create a unique index on the column category.

03:08.030 --> 03:09.350
Let's go and try it.

03:09.350 --> 03:21.990
So create unique non-clustered index and let's give it the name index products category On that table

03:22.750 --> 03:24.550
sales products.

03:24.550 --> 03:27.910
And we are targeting the column category.

03:28.110 --> 03:30.190
So let's go and execute it.

03:30.230 --> 03:35.070
Now we will get an error because the category has duplicates.

03:35.070 --> 03:39.870
So if you go and query again our table you can see we have here duplicate values.

03:39.870 --> 03:44.190
And the SQL cannot go and create unique index for this table.

03:44.190 --> 03:46.590
It's too late but you still can create this index.

03:46.590 --> 03:47.950
